<p>Ranges are from the <a href="https://agm.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/Sideflexing-Engineering-manual_Issue-5.1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rexnord FlatTop engineering manual</a> material tables and apply to Rexnord grades. Other makers rate their resins differently, so confirm with the chain supplier. Cost rises with metal chain and with specialty resins such as low-friction, cut-resistant, and anti-static grades. See <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel conveyor guide</a> for alloy details. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Tabletop Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Beverage bottling lines:</strong> Tabletop chains are the standard conveyor between fillers, cappers, labelers, and case packers in beverage plants. Side-flexing chains route bottles through complex floor plans with multiple turns. Stainless steel chains handle pasteurizer tunnel heat. Low-friction plastic chains reduce noise in high-speed bottle lines.</p>
<p><strong>Dairy and juice:</strong> Tabletop chains transport milk cartons, juice bottles, and yogurt cups through filling, sealing, coding, and case packing. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary chain designs</a> with flush surfaces and open hinge areas allow thorough CIP cleaning. PP and PE chain materials resist the acidic and alkaline cleaning chemicals used in dairy sanitation.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical packaging:</strong> Tabletop chains convey bottles, vials, blisters, and cartons through pharmaceutical packaging lines. The smooth, stable surface prevents container tipping. Controlled accumulation zones use <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure</a> principles on tabletop chains by controlling motor zones independently. Cleanroom-compatible chain materials support cGMP environments.</p>
<p><strong>Can handling:</strong> Empty and filled aluminum and steel cans ride tabletop chains through filling, seaming, coding, and packing operations. The flat chain surface provides stable support for tall, light empty cans that tip easily on roller conveyors. Magnetic inserts in the chain hold steel cans during incline transport.</p>
<p><strong>Carton and case handling:</strong> Mat top conveyors transport folded cartons, filled cases, and trays between forming, filling, sealing, and palletizing equipment. The wider mat top surface handles large cartons and cases. Raised-rib surfaces prevent carton sliding on inclines. <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Collating and laning</a> operations use multiple parallel mat top lanes to organize products before case packing.</p>
<p><strong>Personal care and cosmetics:</strong> Tabletop chains handle bottles, tubes, jars, and compacts through filling, capping, labeling, and cartoning. The layouts typically involve many direction changes in compact floor plans, making side-flexing chain essential. Multi-flex chains handle elevation changes between equipment platforms.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Chain Pull &amp; Length Limits</h3>
<p>Every tabletop chain has a maximum allowable chain pull (tension) that limits the total conveyor length per drive. Longer runs, heavier loads, and tighter curves increase chain pull. Exceeding maximum chain pull causes chain stretch, premature wear, and drive overload. Heavily loaded runs with several curves reach the limit at a much shorter length than lightly loaded straight runs. Calculate chain pull for each conveyor using the manufacturer's method, such as the chain pull calculations in the <a href="https://www.regalrexnord.com.br/uploads/pvt5xbp6ou4pdgwifiqv.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rexnord TableTop and MatTop engineering manual</a>.</p>
<h3>Curve Design</h3>
<p>Side-flexing curves increase chain pull significantly compared to straight runs. The chain must overcome additional friction from the inside-edge guide and the differential speed between the inner and outer chain edges. Larger radius curves generate less friction than tight curves. Curve wear strips should be low-friction UHMW-PE material. Multiple tight curves in sequence may require intermediate drives to keep chain pull within limits.</p>
<h3>Lubrication &amp; Wear</h3>
<p>Tabletop chains ride on wear strips. Rexnord recommends UHMW polyethylene wear strips for most applications and stainless steel wear strips in abrasive environments such as broken glass. The chain-to-wear-strip interface generates friction that determines chain pull, wear rate, and noise level. Dry-running chains use self-lubricating chain materials (typically acetal with internal lubricant additives). Wet-running chains use spray lubrication (food-grade silicone or soap solution) to reduce friction and noise in high-speed beverage lines. Rexnord recommends lubrication whenever the application permits, because it lowers chain tension and extends chain and wear strip life. Over-lubrication causes product contamination and floor hazards. Under-lubrication accelerates chain and wear strip wear.</p>
<h3>Noise Reduction</h3>
<p>Tabletop chain conveyors in bottling plants can be loud enough to put workers above <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.95" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA's 85 dBA eight-hour action level</a> for hearing conservation. Sources include chain-on-wear-strip friction, product-on-chain contact, and container-to-container contact. Noise reduction strategies include: low-noise chain designs with built-in dampening, proper lubrication, slower chain speeds through <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> zones, noise-dampening guide rail materials, and acoustic enclosures around high-noise sections.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Maintenance Guide</h2>
<p><strong>Chain tension:</strong> Check chain catenary on a regular schedule. Tabletop chains stretch over time from wear at hinge points. Excess slack causes chain ride-up on sprockets, tracking problems, and jamming at curves. Adjust take-up to maintain proper catenary on the return side. Replace the chain at the manufacturer's elongation limit. Rexnord's engineering manual uses 3% pitch elongation as a general rule of thumb, after which the chain starts to skip teeth on the drive sprocket.</p>
<p><strong>Wear strip replacement:</strong> UHMW wear strips under the chain wear down, increasing friction, chain pull, and noise. Replace wear strips when they show worn grooves or when chain pull increases noticeably. Curve wear strips wear faster than straight-section strips and require more frequent replacement.</p>
<p><strong>Sprocket inspection:</strong> Worn sprockets cause poor chain engagement, increased noise, and chain jump. Inspect sprocket teeth on a set schedule for wear, chipping, and uneven patterns. Rexnord recommends installing new sprockets whenever new chain is installed. Drive sprockets wear faster than idler sprockets.</p>
<p><strong>Cleaning (food applications):</strong> Tabletop chains in food and beverage plants require daily CIP or manual cleaning. The hinge areas and wear strip channels collect product residue. Hinged side-frames that swing open provide access for cleaning both sides of the chain and the return path. Flush-grid and open-hinge chain designs reduce bacterial harborage.</p>
